---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { PHONE, PHONE_TEL } from '../../lib/site.ts';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Why hire us"
  description="Why pay for an independent foundation second opinion in Arizona? vs free inspections, vs repair-company surveys, vs engineering firms."
>
  <div class="container">
    <h1 class="page-title">Why hire us</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      You're not paying for a free visit that leads to a repair bid. You're paying for an independent
      read so you can slow down — or move forward — with clearer eyes.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>vs. a free foundation inspection</h2>
      <p>
        Free evaluations are not free to perform. Someone pays — usually through repair volume and
        close rates. A free inspection can be thorough and honest. It is still usually the front end
        of a repair business. Options like <em>monitor</em>, <em>fix drainage first</em>, or
        <em>this may never get worse</em> often get less airtime than the path that funds the visit.
      </p>
      <p>
        You hire us once. The visit is complete when you understand the evidence — whether that
        points to monitoring, drainage, repair, or a PE.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>vs. a floor survey from a repair company</h2>
      <p>
        Many foundation repair companies can shoot floor elevations — sometimes for a fee, sometimes
        as part of a proposal visit. The measurement may be real. The next conversation is still
        usually about whether to buy a repair.
      </p>
      <p>
        We measure independently and stop at advice. If repair is warranted, we'll say so — with
        credibility, because we have no stake in the steel. If it isn't, we'll say that too. Honest
        contractors benefit either way: you send them better-informed clients, and the right jobs
        still get done. Reputable repair contractors are often the right next step; we help you get
        there with better questions, not a competing bid.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>vs. hiring an engineering firm first</h2>
      <p>
        Geotechnical and structural engineers do essential work — especially when design, stamps, or
        litigation matter. Many firms won't drive to Show Low for $1,250, and stamped reports cost
        more than a homeowner often needs on day one.
      </p>
      <p>
        We fill the gap: practical field measurement and honest guidance. When an Arizona PE is the
        right next step, we'll tell you plainly.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What you're buying</h2>
      <table class="compare">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>Foundation Repair Advocate</th>
            <th>Typical sales visit</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Fee</td>
            <td>Paid upfront — clarity is the product</td>
            <td>"Free" — recovered through repair</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Goal</td>
            <td>Independent path: monitor, drainage, PE, or repair when warranted</td>
            <td>Typically a repair proposal</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>"Wait and monitor"</td>
            <td>Said when supported</td>
            <td>Rare</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Repair sold</td>
            <td>Never</td>
            <td>Core business</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>25 years in foundation repair and geotechnical field work.</strong> We translate
          what the industry knows into what homeowners need to hear to decide — including when a
          good contractor should do the work.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`tel:${PHONE_TEL}`}>Talk it through — {PHONE}</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
